speechtext:
I think it important, coming from Nova Scotia, that this matter be given some thought. The question of rentals may be raised. But here we have in Canada eighteen reserve divisions; and I believe every province, with the exception of Nova Scotia, has a division of the Royal Canadian Naval Volunteer Reserve. We have just heard the hon. member for Vancouver South (Mr. Green) point out that men should be trained with a view to entering the merchant marine. The men who have been in the reserve unit have the first chance, because they have had training. They form groups;
(Mr. A. L. Macdonald.]
young men talk to each other, and we find these units throughout Canada, except in the province surrounded by the sea, where one would expect to find them. After the war is over the other provinces are going to have the advantage, when one would expect men for the merchant marine to come from Nova Scotia. I urge upon the minister that serious thought be given to placing Nova Scotia in the same position as other provinces.
